Based on my radiographic survey of the panoramic image, the most plausible periapical lesions are in the lower molar/premolar regions with gross caries or restorations, plus the restored upper-left molar.

```json
{"lesions": [
  {"box": [0.224, 0.551, 0.287, 0.687], "pai": 4},
  {"box": [0.619, 0.607, 0.696, 0.775], "pai": 4},
  {"box": [0.655, 0.343, 0.744, 0.487], "pai": 3}
]}
```

Caveats worth stating plainly: this panoramic is low-resolution and the periapical bone is partly confounded by the maxillary sinuses, metal restorations (bright bloom) and the mandibular canal. The two mandibular boxes sit at teeth with gross caries/multiple restorations, where apical rarefaction is most likely; the maxillary box abuts the sinus shadow, so its PAI 3 is the least certain of the three. I could not confidently identify any additional well-defined lesions in the anterior or contralateral segments.
